{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RE-SCOPED — my original filing was wrong on both counts. Corrected here.\n\nA peer session challenged this ticket, I verified their claims, and they are right. Recording the\nfull correction rather than quietly editing, because this ticket was filed P1 and I propagated its\nfalse premise to other agents.\n\n### What I got wrong\n\n**1. `--caused-by` is NOT absent or inert. It works.**\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:123-124,150-152,164` — `_parse_flags` parses\n  `[--caused-by[=]]` and returns it; documented as the explicit culprit (ticket 555e).\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_caused_by_autoderive.py:33`\n  `test_explicit_caused_by_on_bug_close` drives `--caused-by={culprit}` **through the CLI** and\n  asserts the resulting edge. It is green.\n- The peer demonstrated it directly in a fresh store with **zero commits and no `file_impact`**,\n  so `blame.derive_caused_by` was structurally incapable of producing anything — and the edge\n  still named the flag's culprit.\n\n**2. My \"silently ignores unknown flags\" experiment was invalid.**\n\nI ran `rebar transition <id> open open --definitely-not-a-real-flag` against an already-open\nticket. That returns `no change: … already open` from an **early return**, before any close-path\nflag handling. It never exercised a close, which is the only path where `--caused-by` does\nanything. A bogus flag and a working flag behave identically on a no-op, so the experiment could\nnot distinguish them. Separately, `_parse_flags` documents that unknown tokens are skipped **by\ndesign**, so that half is intended behaviour, not a defect.\n\n### What IS real: two help sources for one verb, drifted apart\n\n- `src/rebar/_cli/help/transition.txt` is what `rebar transition --help` renders. It contains\n  **0** occurrences of `caused`.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:65` contains a SECOND help blob that documents it:\n  `\"  --caused-by=<id>         On a bug close, draw a caused_by link to the culprit change/ticket (overrides git-blame auto-derivation).\"`\n\nBoth live in `src/rebar`. The commit that added the flag — `0f999860ad` (2026-07-19, ticket\n`555e-3efa-4ad6-4169`, \"555e: blame-derived caused_by on bug close + --caused-by\") — added the\nflag, the parser, the tests, and the `transition.py` help line, and did **not** update\n`_cli/help/transition.txt`.\n\nVerified this is not staleness on my side: the help line landed 2026-07-19 and my installed binary\nis from today.\n\n### Why it matters, at its true (lower) severity\n\nThe flag works, so nothing is broken — the cost is **discoverability**. An agent reading\n`rebar transition --help` sees no `--caused-by` and reasonably concludes it does not exist. That is\nexactly what happened to me, and I then propagated the wrong conclusion into instructions to four\nother agents and into two other tickets. `docs/user-guide.md:194`, `docs/clone-guidance.md` and the\n`/rebar-debug` skill all instruct passing it, so the rendered help is the odd one out.\n\nThis is the same shape as most of today's other findings: a change created an obligation (keep the\ntwo help sources in step) and discharged it on one site only. It is **not** a correctness bug, and\nP1 was far too high. Lowering to **P3**.\n\n### Corrected Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar transition --help` documents `--caused-by=<id>`, matching the text already in\n      `_commands/transition.py:65` — verified by a test asserting the rendered help contains it.\n- [ ] The duplication is addressed at the root, not by copying the line into a second file: either\n      one help source is derived from the other, or a test asserts the two cannot diverge for any\n      flag the parser accepts. Copying the string leaves the next flag free to drift the same way.\n- [ ] Every other flag `_parse_flags` accepts is present in the rendered help (`--reason`,\n      `--class`, `--force`, `--ref`, `--caused-by`) — verified by a test derived from the parser\n      rather than a hand-written list.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n      (`new=0 increased=0 stale=0`) pass.\n\n### Withdrawn from this ticket\n\nThe unknown-flag-rejection criteria are removed: silent skipping is documented, intended behaviour,\nand my evidence for it being harmful was an invalid experiment. If anyone wants to argue unknown\nflags should be rejected, that is a separate design proposal and needs its own case."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787270848660471001,"uuid":"ae83ef7c-e358-459b-b2f1-cc04942781a3"}